# Copy to .env at the repository root and fill in real values.
#   PowerShell:  Copy-Item .env.example .env
#   cmd:         copy .env.example .env
#
# .env is gitignored. This file is the committed record of WHICH keys exist,
# never of their values.
#
# The same key names are what a deployed environment supplies as environment
# variables — Azure App Service Application Settings, Container Apps secrets, or
# a Kubernetes Secret. There is no .env in those environments: the imports in
# application.yml are optional, so they skip and the environment wins. Deploying
# means copying the keys below into the host's settings, not shipping this file.

# --- Required ---

# Signs and verifies JWT access tokens. At least 32 characters: HS256 needs a
# 256-bit key and the app refuses to start on anything shorter. Any value works
# locally and it need not match what teammates use, but changing it invalidates
# every token already issued.
JWT_SECRET=change-me-to-at-least-32-characters

# --- Optional: defaults in application.yml already match docker-compose ---

# KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS=localhost:9092

# Leave these unset to use the local Postgres from docker-compose.yml. Set all
# three to point at a hosted database instead.
#
# Azure Database for PostgreSQL:
#   - sslmode=require is mandatory; the connection is refused without it
#   - add a firewall rule for your client IP under Networking, or the connect
#     call times out rather than failing with a useful message
#   - DB_USER is the plain admin name on Flexible Server (no @servername suffix,
#     which was the older Single Server format)
#
# DB_URL=jdbc:postgresql://<server>.postgres.database.azure.com:5432/crm?sslmode=require
# DB_USER=<admin-user>
# DB_PASSWORD=<password>
